multiplies.l4:18:1-37:
  `fixed chaser` can start itself again: a HENCE or LEST in it leads back to `fixed chaser`, and I could not check that this comes to an end, because one of its deadlines is counted from a fixed moment (WITHIN … OF …), and once that moment is past, each new copy is already late.
  Its author says why it does, with the @ends-because above `fixed chaser`:
  
    Alex pays before day 2
  
  So L4 does not warn about it. That reason is what a reader should check.
multiplies.l4:24:10-46:
  `fixed chaser` starts 2 copies of itself at once: its LEST joins them with RAND.
  So every time a deadline is missed, the number of copies open doubles.
  Nothing written limits how many times round it goes.
  
  If one copy is what you meant, start it once.
  
  @ends-because does not silence this warning: L4 has checked that the copies multiply, and the mark answers only the warning that L4 could not check that a rule comes to an end.
