  If one copy is what you meant, start it once.
multiplies.l4:51:1-35:
  The @ends-because above `first chaser` says why it comes to an end:
  
    Alex pays in the end
  
  It silences nothing: the warning shown on `second chaser` is about something L4 has checked, and the mark answers only the warning that L4 could not check that a rule comes to an end.
multiplies.l4:64:10-46:
  `second chaser` starts 2 copies of rules that lead back to it at once: its LEST joins them with RAND.
  The rules that lead back to each other are `second chaser` and `first chaser`.
  So every time a deadline is missed, the number of copies open doubles.
  Nothing written limits how many times round it goes.
  
  If one copy is what you meant, start it once.
  
  @ends-because does not silence this warning: L4 has checked that the copies multiply, and the mark answers only the warning that L4 could not check that a rule comes to an end.
multiplies.l4:75:15-83:
  `spreading chaser` starts 2 copies of itself at once: its LEST joins them with RAND.
  So every time a deadline is missed, the number of copies open doubles.
  Each copy is given `n` less 1, so if `n` reaches a stop, this comes to an end, but only after the number open has doubled at every step down: as many as 2 to the power of `n`.
  
  If one copy is what you meant, start it once.
multiplies.l4:86:15-77:
  `faster chaser` starts 2 copies of itself at once: its LEST joins them with RAND.
  So every time a deadline is missed, the number of copies open doubles.
  Each copy is given `n` less 2, so if `n` reaches a stop, this comes to an end, but only after the number open has doubled at every step down: as many as 2 to the power of (`n` divided by 2).
  
  If one copy is what you meant, start it once.
